Run a Curry program from Python
===============================

This example is the first contact with the Python API.  The script go.py
imports the Curry module rev.curry and prints the values of its goal.


1. What the example shows about functional logic programming
-------------------------------------------------------------

The module rev.curry defines list reversal with two functions:

    append :: [t] -> [t] -> [t]
    append []     x  = x
    append (x:xs) ys = x : append xs ys

    rev :: [t] -> [t]
    rev []     = []
    rev (x:xs) = append (rev xs) [x]

The goal main is rev [1,2,3,4].  The functions are deterministic, so the goal
has one value.  A Curry goal may have several values, and the Python side is
written for that case.


2. What the example shows about calling Curry from Python
----------------------------------------------------------

The script adds its own directory to curry.path, the search path for Curry
modules.  Then it imports the module from the virtual package curry.lib:

    from curry.lib import rev

The import compiles rev.curry and loads it.  The functions of the module are
attributes of the module object.  The call

    curry.eval(rev.main)

returns an iterator over the values of the goal.  The script prints each
value.  A Curry value prints in Curry syntax, so a list of Ints prints as
[4, 3, 2, 1].


3. How to run
-------------

Run "make stage" from the root of the repository first.  Then run

    ./run

The run script uses the C++ backend by default.  To pick a backend, set
SPRITE_INTERPRETER_FLAGS in the environment:

    SPRITE_INTERPRETER_FLAGS=backend:py ./run
    SPRITE_INTERPRETER_FLAGS=backend:cxx ./run


4. Expected output
------------------

The file expected.out holds the exact output of ./run:

    % python go.py
    [4, 3, 2, 1]

The output is identical on both backends.


5. Limits
---------

A warm run of ./run took 0.4 s on the Python backend and 0.2 s on the C++
backend.  The first run also compiles the module with the Curry front end,
which takes several seconds more.
